        <fig id="article-25794.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Microscopic Anatomy of the Hypophysis. Light micrograph at 10 times magnification demonstrates the distinct cellular regions of the hypophysis. The pars distalis represents the glandular anterior lobe, characterized by a dense population of chromophils and chromophobes. Centrally located, the pars intermedia contains follicles filled with colloid, serving as the boundary between the anterior and posterior sections. The pars nervosa appears as a paler, fibrous region composed of unmyelinated axons and pituicytes, forming the bulk of the posterior lobe. Contributed by Wikimedia Commons (CC by 4.0) https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Pituitary_gland_histology_2014" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
